[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s Linux firewall (netfilter/iptables) in the forwarding path. Mechanism: Inter-site traffic from the transit link is being dropped by FORWARD chain rules on router6, so hosts in 172.16.10.0/24 can reach router6’s interfaces but not the end hosts behind it.

Evidence and localization:
- From PC4/PC5, pings to Location C hosts (e.g., 172.18.51.15) time out, but pings to router6’s interfaces 172.18.50.254 and 172.18.51.254 succeed, proving L3 reachability to router6 and failure only for forwarded traffic.
- Router0 has correct RIP routes toward 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.16.1.2, and router6 itself can ping 172.18.51.15 successfully, isolating the issue to router6’s forwarding path, not routing.
- On router6, iptables shows:
  - ACCEPT only tcp dpt 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1 via -i eth2
  - Followed by blanket DROP for all protocols on -i eth2 (the transit interface 10.50.1.2)
  - Packet counters confirm hits: DROP on eth2 shows 51 packets dropped during testing.

Root cause: A misconfigured/leftover firewall policy on router6’s FORWARD chain that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, except a narrow TCP/80 exception, blocking inter-site connectivity to Location C.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust router6’s FORWARD rules to permit legitimate inter-site forwarding. For example:
- iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP (remove blanket drop rules)
- iptables -F FORWARD (if no filtering is needed)
- Then add explicit allows as required (e.g., iptables -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j ACCEPT; iptables -A FORWARD -o eth2 -j ACCEPT).
After this, PC4/PC5 should regain reachability to 172.18.51.0/24 and other networks behind router6.
=== END ===